(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endocrine-physiology-1-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology I: Pituitary & Thyroid — Quiz Set 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which hypothalamic-hypophysial regulatory hormone stimulates TSH secretion by thyrotropes?","choices":["Corticotropin-releasing hormone","Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Growth hormone-releasing hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), a peptide, stimulates TSH secretion by thyrotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic-hypophysial regulatory hormone stimulates ACTH secretion by corticotropes?","choices":["Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Corticotropin-releasing hormone","Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone","Prolactin-inhibiting hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), a peptide, stimulates ACTH secretion by corticotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Besides growth hormone-releasing hormone, which other hypothalamic factor specifically regulates GH secretion?","choices":["Corticotropin-releasing hormone","Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone","Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone is the other hypothalamic peptide that specifically regulates GH secretion, inhibiting it (opposite to GHRH's stimulatory role). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Control of hormone secretion in this lecture is described as including negative feedback, positive feedback, cyclic variations, and:","choices":["Homeostasis","Random fluctuation with no pattern","Exclusively genetic control with no physiological regulation","Permanent suppression after puberty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Homeostasis is listed alongside negative feedback, positive feedback, and cyclic variations as part of hormone secretion control. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Regulatory hormones from the hypothalamus reach the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) via which route?","choices":["Diffusion through cerebrospinal fluid","Direct nerve fiber connections only","Systemic arterial circulation only","The hypophyseal portal system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Regulatory hormones are released into the hypophyseal portal system for delivery specifically to the anterior lobe of the pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"According to the lecture's hypothalamic control diagram, which posterior pituitary target organ list is correct for the ADH pathway?","choices":["Gonads","Adrenal cortex","Thyroid gland","Kidneys"],"answer":3,"exp":"ADH's target organ shown in the hypothalamic control diagram is the kidneys. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which hormone, released by neurons under direct hypothalamic control (bypassing the anterior pituitary regulatory-hormone route), acts on uterine smooth muscle and mammary glands in females?","choices":["Oxytocin","Follitropin","Corticotropin","Thyrotropin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxytocin, released directly from the posterior pituitary under direct hypothalamic neural control, acts on uterine smooth muscle and mammary glands in females (and on ductus deferens/prostate smooth muscle in males). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Per the lecture's histologic staining classification, which anterior pituitary cell types are acidophilic?","choices":["Corticotropes and thyrotropes","Somatotropes and mammotropes","Gonadotropes and corticotropes","Thyrotropes and gonadotropes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Somatotropes and mammotropes (lactotropes) are classified as acidophilic cell types. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Per the lecture's histologic staining classification, which anterior pituitary cell types are basophilic?","choices":["Somatotropes and corticotropes only","Somatotropes, mammotropes, and thyrotropes","Thyrotropes, gonadotropes, and corticotropes","Mammotropes and gonadotropes only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Thyrotropes, gonadotropes, and corticotropes are classified as basophilic cell types. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Mammotropes secrete prolactin, whose primary function is to:","choices":["Stimulate glucocorticoid secretion from the adrenal cortex","Stimulate body growth","Stimulate milk production and secretion","Stimulate spermatogenesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Prolactin, secreted by mammotropes, stimulates milk production and secretion. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Thyrotropes secrete TSH, whose function is to:","choices":["Stimulate milk production","Stimulate secretion of glucocorticoids from the adrenal cortex","Stimulate spermatogenesis in the testis","Stimulate production of thyroid hormone by follicular cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"TSH stimulates production of thyroid hormone by the thyroid's follicular cells. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Gonadotropes secrete FSH and LH. Which combined actions are these hormones responsible for?","choices":["Stimulating follicular cells in the ovary/spermatogenesis in the testis, and stimulating sex-steroid production from the gonads","Stimulating the release of glucocorticoid and androgen hormones specifically from cells of the adrenal cortex.","Stimulating increased hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogen breakdown in the liver to raise blood glucose.","Stimulating thyroid follicular cell activity and directly stimulating milk production from the mammary glands."],"answer":0,"exp":"FSH and LH stimulate follicular cells in the ovary and spermatogenesis in the testis, and stimulate production of estrogen/progesterone from the ovary and testosterone from the testis. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Corticotropes secrete ACTH, which stimulates secretion of which substances from the adrenal cortex?","choices":["Glucocorticoids and androgens","Catecholamines only","Thyroid hormones","Mineralocorticoids exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"ACTH stimulates secretion of glucocorticoids and androgens from the adrenal cortex. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which nine peptide/protein hormones make up the 'KEY TO PITUITARY HORMONES' listed together in this lecture's overview figure?","choices":["Insulin, glucagon, and PTH plus the six anterior hormones","ACTH, TSH, GH, PRL, FSH, LH, MSH, ADH, and Oxytocin","Only ACTH, TSH, and GH","Cortisol, aldosterone, and the six anterior hormones"],"answer":1,"exp":"The overview figure's key lists ACTH, TSH, GH, PRL, FSH, LH, MSH (all anterior), plus ADH and oxytocin (posterior) as the full set of pituitary hormones. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"The growth hormone receptor is best classified as which type of receptor?","choices":["A nuclear steroid receptor","A G-protein-coupled receptor exclusively","An ion channel receptor","An enzyme-linked receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"The growth hormone receptor is enzyme-linked. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does growth hormone increase protein synthesis at the cellular level?","choices":["By having no measurable effect at all on gene transcription, RNA translation, or protein catabolism.","By increasing protein catabolism exclusively while leaving transcription and translation unchanged.","By increasing amino acid transport, RNA translation rate, and transcription rate, while decreasing protein catabolism","By decreasing amino acid transport into cells and decreasing the overall rate of RNA translation."],"answer":2,"exp":"GH increases protein synthesis by increasing amino acid transport into cells, increasing the rate of RNA translation, increasing the rate of transcription, and decreasing protein catabolism. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"The decrease in GH-driven protein catabolism is suggested to be related to which other GH metabolic action?","choices":["Decreased amino acid transport","Increased fat utilization","Increased carbohydrate utilization","Decreased RNA transcription"],"answer":1,"exp":"The decrease in protein catabolism under GH is suggested to possibly be related to GH's increased fat utilization (sparing protein by favoring fat as an energy source). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Growth hormone's carbohydrate-sparing, fat-mobilizing metabolic profile is sometimes described using which term, reflecting its tendency to raise blood glucose?","choices":["Diabetogenic effects","Natriuretic effects","Anabolic effects","Anti-inflammatory effects"],"answer":0,"exp":"GH's metabolic actions (decreased carbohydrate utilization, increased gluconeogenesis) are described using the term 'diabetogenic effects,' reflecting the tendency to raise blood glucose. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which figure/study is referenced to demonstrate that growth hormone promotes growth?","choices":["A study in adult humans only","A study in rats","No experimental evidence is cited","A study in zebrafish"],"answer":1,"exp":"A study in rats is referenced to demonstrate GH's growth-promoting effect. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the somatomedin hypothesis pathway, growth hormone acts on the liver to stimulate release of which growth-mediating factor?","choices":["Insulin","IGF-1","Cortisol","Thyroxine"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the somatomedin hypothesis, GH stimulates the liver to release IGF-1, which mediates many of GH's peripheral growth-promoting effects. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"The 'Metabolic Actions of Growth Hormone Summary' groups GH's effects into which three categories, consistent with earlier slides?","choices":["Bone, muscle, skin","Water, electrolyte, acid-base","Protein, fat, carbohydrate","Cardiac, renal, hepatic"],"answer":2,"exp":"GH's metabolic actions are consistently summarized around protein synthesis, fat utilization, and carbohydrate utilization. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Growth hormone stimulates osteoblasts as part of its bone-growth actions. What is the functional role of osteoblasts?","choices":["Red blood cell destruction","Fat storage within bone marrow","Bone resorption","Bone formation/deposition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Osteoblasts are bone-forming cells, responsible for new bone deposition — consistent with GH's growth-promoting role. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which cells are described as supportive glial-like cells of the posterior pituitary?","choices":["Pituicytes","Chondrocytes","Podocytes","Oxyphil cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pituicytes are the supportive cells of the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"The two major hormones of the posterior pituitary are ADH and:","choices":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","TSH","ACTH"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin are the two major posterior pituitary hormones. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"ADH's alternate name, reflecting its vasoactive properties, is:","choices":["Arginine vasopressin","Thyroxine-releasing peptide","Corticotropin analog","Somatostatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADH is also called arginine vasopressin (AVP), reflecting its vasoconstrictive/pressor properties. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where in the hypothalamus is oxytocin primarily produced?","choices":["Paraventricular nucleus","Ventromedial nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Supraoptic nuclei exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxytocin is produced in the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Oxytocin's two described physiological functions are milk let-down and:","choices":["Uterine contraction","Bone growth","Thyroid hormone synthesis","Water reabsorption in the kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxytocin's two described functions are milk let-down and uterine contraction. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"At high T3/T4 levels, protein metabolism shows which combination of effects?","choices":["No measurable effect on either protein synthesis or protein breakdown","Increased synthesis with no change in proteolysis","Increased synthesis and increased proteolysis (muscle wasting)","Decreased synthesis and decreased proteolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"High T3/T4 increases both protein synthesis AND proteolysis, with the increased proteolysis contributing to muscle wasting. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What happens to mean arterial pressure and pulse pressure under increased thyroid hormone?","choices":["Mean pressure is largely unchanged, while pulse pressure increases","Both mean arterial pressure and pulse pressure decrease substantially","Mean pressure decreases while pulse pressure is unchanged","Both mean pressure and pulse pressure increase substantially"],"answer":0,"exp":"Under increased thyroid hormone, mean arterial pressure is largely unchanged, while pulse pressure increases, alongside increased cardiac output, tissue blood flow, and heart rate. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Large increases in thyroid hormone can decrease heart strength due to:","choices":["Excessive protein synthesis only, with no catabolism","Complete cessation of cardiac contraction","Decreased cardiac output","Excessive protein catabolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Large increases in thyroid hormone can decrease heart strength due to excessive protein catabolism, in contrast to the mild strength-increasing effect seen at slight increases in thyroid hormone. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 73"}]};
